<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_flux-induction" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>Flux and Induction</title>

	<assemblage component="implementation">
  <title>Implementation Notes</title>
  <p>
	The activities in this section and the previous section (<xref ref="sec_current-loops"/>) were designed to be implemented in an interactive studio classroom, after students have completed the previous sections in the chapter.  They take about two hours.
	</p>
  </assemblage>

<subsubsection>
	<title>Activities</title>
  </subsubsection>

<activity>
	<title>Flux Loops</title>
	<p>
		
	</p>
	<p>
		A square loop (side length <m>s</m>) in the plane of the screen is in a region of magnetic field.  Find the magnetic flux through the loop if:
		<ol>
			<li>
				<p>
					The magnetic field is a uniform <m>B_o</m> and points into the screen.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					The magnetic field is a uniform <m>B_o</m> and points to the right.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					The magnetic field points into the screen and increases linearly from <m>0</m> along the left edge to <m>B_o</m> along the right edge.
				</p>
			</li>
		</ol>
	</p>

</activity>

<activity>
	<title>The Pickup Coil</title>
	<introduction>
		<p>
	
	</p>
	<p>
		Run this <url href="beav.es/pickup-coil">simulation</url>.  Go to the Pickup Coil tab.
	</p>
	</introduction>
	
	<task>
			<p>
				Play with the simulation for a few minutes.  Record a list of observations.
			</p>
		</task>
		<task>
			<p>
				How many different kinds of changes can you make that will cause the light bulb to light up?
			</p>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Consider the statement: <q>While the magnetic flux through the loop changes, there is current induced in the loop.</q>  What evidence is there to support this statement?  Can you find any evidence to refute the statement?  Can you find any evidence that suggests the statement is incomplete?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					 Identify what properties the induced current in the loop depends on.  Justify your answers with evidence from the simulation.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
</activity>

<activity>
	<title>Force on a Circuit</title>
	<introduction>
		<p>
	
	</p>
	<p>
		The figure below shows a small, square conducting loop with side length <m>s</m> being pushed to the right at constant speed <m>v</m> toward a region of uniform magnetic field <m>B</m> pointing outward.
	</p>

	<figure xml:id="fig_loop-push">
			<title>Loop Push Figure</title>
				<caption>A square loop moving into a magnetic field.</caption>
				<image source="Loop-Push.png" width = "60%" ><description>Left: a square loop moving to the right at speed v.  Right: a region of uniform magnetic field pointing out of the screen.</description></image>
			</figure>

	</introduction>
	
	<task>
			<p>
				Choose an area vector for the wire loop.  Use the right-hand rule to determine whether a clockwise current in the loop is positive or negative.
			</p>
		</task>
		<task>
			<p>
				Consider a positive charge located somewhere in the wire.  Will this positive charge experience a magnetic force?  If so, what direction?
			</p>
		</task>
		<task>
			<p>
				Predict whether or not the force you identified above will be able to drive a current through the loop during each of the following intervals:
			</p>
			<ol marker="A.">
				<li>
					<p>
						As the loop is entering the field.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						While the loop is entirely within the field.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						As the loop is exiting the field.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ol>
		</task>
		<task>
			<p>
				Sketch and label a graph of the magnetic flux <m>\Phi_B</m> as a function of time.
			</p>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Sketch and label a graph of the current through the loop as a function of time.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
</activity>

<definition xml:id="mag_induction">
	<title>Magnetic Induction</title>
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>
			A changing magnetic flux through a conducting loop will <alert>induce</alert> a current!
		</p>
		<p>
			<me>I_{\text{induced}} \propto - \frac{\partial \Phi_B}{\partial t}</me>
		</p>
	</statement>
</definition>



<!--NO AI-->
</section>

